# The General Court lane, one step per line, run in order by watchers/gc_lane.py.
# A step is what follows "python3". "handover " in front means the step takes
# archive/.lock itself. A line is run once; logs/gc_lane.done lists what has run.
# Add a step only once its fetcher has been read for the one-worker rules.

# 2015-2016: the 883 bills the database's Docket view does not reach. Stopped
# at 09:51 on the 11th, 102 pages in, when a review found this fetcher cached
# the firewall's block page and missed a reset wrapped in a URLError; the step
# below supersedes it, and the 102 pages are cached.
# fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2015-2016 --seed Docket_db_2015-2016.txt --delay 15 --stop-refused 2

# 2015-2016, with the seed corrected. The database's "2016" is not 2016: its
# 1,962 SessionYear-2016 rows are all dated 2015, the first-year history of
# 190 bills carried over, and two of them carry another bill's LSR. This seed
# (Docket_db_2015.txt) keeps only session-2015 rows of bills data/bills.json
# files under 2015, with the bill's own LSR: 8,322 rows on 716 bills. Every
# one of the 1,072 bills filed under 2016 comes from its own page; those
# already cached are not asked again.
# Stopped at 11:36 on the 11th, 381 of the 1,072 cached, on two 60-second read
# timeouts 97 minutes in -- the second run in a row to slow or stop about
# ninety minutes into business hours. netcheck at 12:37 found the address
# answering as it always has. Resumed at 25s apart rather than 15.
# fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2015-2016 --seed Docket_db_2015.txt --delay 15 --stop-refused 2
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2015-2016 --seed Docket_db_2015.txt --delay 25 --stop-refused 2
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-docket

# EMPTY DOCKET PAGES, asked again. 17 cached pages of 2017-2022 carry the
# bill's title and no docket row; a person opened SB 68 of 2017 on the 11th
# and saw twelve rows ending in its signature and chapter 35. With
# --refetch-empty a run asks for those pages and for any bill never cached
# (12 more), and nothing else: about 29 requests across the three terms.
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2017-2018 --delay 25 --stop-refused 2 --refetch-empty
watchers/rest.py 600 after-refetch-2017-2018
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2019-2020 --delay 25 --stop-refused 2 --refetch-empty
watchers/rest.py 600 after-refetch-2019-2020
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2021-2022 --delay 25 --stop-refused 2 --refetch-empty
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-refetch-2021-2022

# BILL TEXT, 2024 BACK TO 1989, newest first -- the person's order of the
# 11th: the modern terms are the useful ones, and all of it eventually.
# --plan: 30,049 requests for bills and constitutional amendments, 954
# more for resolutions and the rest. Runs of 800 at 20s apart, an hour's
# rest between; each run skips what is on disk, so the same range queued
# again carries on where the last stopped.
#
# A short run first on 2024: the application's path (stored id with sy),
# so the LSR check meets its pages before it meets 800 of them.
# Superseded, in the old oldest-first order:
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2021 --to 2021 --kinds HB,SB --budget 20 --note proving
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2017 --to 2021 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --budget 800 --note 2017-21-a
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2023 --to 2024 --budget 25 --note 2023-24-proving
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2023 --to 2024 --budget 800 --note 2023-24-a
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2017 --to 2021 --kinds HR,SR,HCR,SCR,HJR,SJR --budget 400 --note 2017-21-resolutions
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2024 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB --delay 20 --budget 25 --note proving-2024
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-proving-2024
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 1989 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --newest-first --delay 20 --budget 800 --note bills-01
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-bills-01
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 1989 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --newest-first --delay 20 --budget 800 --note bills-02
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-bills-02
